    {
      "id": "pupw6pn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Work is mathematically defined as the product of force and displacement in the direction of the force (W = F * d * cos θ).",
        "Work can be positive, negative, or zero depending on the angle θ between force and displacement.",
        "Mechanical energy exists in two main forms: Kinetic Energy (KE = 1/2 m v²) and Potential Energy (PE = m g h).",
        "The Law of Conservation of Energy states that total energy in an isolated system remains constant.",
        "Power represents the rate of doing work or energy consumption, measured in Watts (1 W = 1 J/s)."
      ],
      "id": "39s5xoh"
    },
